// A Shard of the Old World (D10), p.20-21.
(
    name: "Shard of the Old World",
    die: D10,
    entries: [
        (1, 1, (title: "A Rusted Crucifix", text: "This is not a symbol of the Choir's faith, but of the faith that came before. It is bent and worthless, and the Savior silent. Its truth is this: God does not sing. God is silent, perhaps dead, but He is not the horror in the sky. It reminds you that divinity was once a source of quiet hope, not a ravenous, all-consuming symphony.")),
        (2, 2, (title: "A Pagan Amulet", text: "A piece of carved wood or smooth river stone, etched with a spiral or a beast's head. It speaks of a time when humanity's gods were of the earth, not the sky or the abyss. Its truth is in the soil, the trees, and the moon. A natural world with its own brutal order, one that has been violated by both the Choir and the Flesh.")),
        (3, 3, (title: "A Child's Drawing", text: "A crude sketch on faded parchment, showing a lopsided house, a dog, and stick-figure people holding hands. It is a portrait of a simple, profound happiness that has been rendered impossible. Its truth is that joy was once simple and attainable. This memory of innocence is a bulwark against the Choir's twisted ecstasy and the Flesh's mindless agony.")),
        (4, 4, (title: "An Untarnished Silver Coin", text: "Stamped with the profile of Robert II, the Fallen King, this coin is useless as currency but priceless as a memory. Its truth is order, law, and society. It is a reminder that humanity once built things—kingdoms, alliances, trade—and was not merely prey for cosmic forces.")),
        (5, 5, (title: "A Page of Plato's Republic", text: "A ripped page from a book, filled with dense, reasoned script questioning the nature of existence. Its arguments are now tragically quaint. Its truth is the power of the human mind to reason and to question. It reminds you that you are a thinking being, not just a vessel for song or a morsel for the abyss.")),
        (6, 6, (title: "A Bag of Seeds", text: "There was a time when this little bag held a promise, an opportunity. Some hard work, good weather, and soil, and a real miracle—not those horrible things taking place now—would produce life. Simple, good life, ready to nurture others.")),
        (7, 7, (title: "A Key to a Burned-Down House", text: "The lock is gone, the home is ashes, but the key remains. It fit a door that offered safety, warmth, and belonging. Its truth is the concept of sanctuary. It is a promise you made to yourself that you will find a place to be safe again, even if you have to build it in the ruins of the world.")),
        (8, 8, (title: "A Shard of Stained Glass", text: "A piece of colored glass from a church window, perhaps showing the blue of a saint's robe or the gold of a halo. The saint's story is forgotten or has been corrupted by the Choir's new gospels. Its truth is that history itself has been violated. You hold a fragment of the true story, a reminder that the past can be murdered, just like a person.")),
        (9, 9, (title: "A Dried, Scentless Flower", text: "A fragile, faded bloom, pressed within a locket or a book's pages. It grew, bloomed, and died without purpose or audience. Its truth is that beauty can exist without a grand, terrible design. It is a symbol of a natural world that was gentle and aimless, a stark contrast to the purposeful horror of the new age.")),
        (10, 10, (title: "A Vial of Sea Water", text: "A small, corked bottle containing saltwater from a coast that has long since been unmade by the Flesh. Its truth is the memory of boundaries. It reminds you that the world once had edges, that the land ended and the sea began. It is a symbol of a finite world, as opposed to the endless, invasive hunger of the Flesh that has erased it all.")),
    ],
)
